Problem One: RAG-enhanced LLM Tool for Network Configuration with Automatic Validation and Error Correction

1. Backgrounds and Motivations
As network infrastructures become increasingly complex, managing and configuring networks manually presents significant challenges for engineers and brings a huge manpower cost for data center maintenance. In a recent paper (i.e., NetConfEval [1]), the authors used LLMs for network configurations based on a straightforward prompt-engineering scheme. However, several limitations remain in NetConfEval.
 
First, if we consider using the LLM-assisted network configuration tool in a data center, where the network to be configured might be used to support high-value enterprise applications, we anticipate zero tolerance for network configuration errors. NetConfEval only reported a simple and non-automatic prompt-based error feedback scheme for enhanced reliability. A highly reliable "code correction" mechanism that can perfectly cooperate with automated network validation tools (such as SwitchV reported in [2]) is desired for the LLM-assisted network configuration process.
 
Further, the multi-step configuration code generation could be very time-consuming (according to my previous experience with LLMs on a similar code generation topic [3]). The authors in NetConfEval claimed that they had stored the generated configuration for future reuse, but they did not give further details on the reusing process. A Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) scheme dealing with past successful configurations could help to cut down the time cost by reusing its "previous memory" on network configuration.
 
Finally, NetConfEval relies on prompt interactions with GPT-4. However, it is important to note that models like GPT-4 are not specifically designed for networking tasks, making the configuration in NetConfEval both inefficient and prone to errors. Additionally, there might be privacy and security concerns when uploading the expected network setup to the commercial platform provided by OpenAI. We are interested in fine-tuning a task-specific LLM to enhance the model's performance in the network configuration task, as well as addressing the privacy and security concerns by deploying these fine-tuned models locally. Furthermore, I believe the data we collect (or generate) for the model fine-tuning will also be a high-impact open-source contribution to the networking community. 

In general, this project proposes an automated network configuration system building upon LLMs. The goal of this project is to streamline the LLM-assisted network configuration code generation process with 1) a topology similarity-based RAG scheme that accelerates the configuration process by partly referring to and re-using previously obtained configuration codes with high similarity in terms of network topology, 2) a validate-revise mechanism that increases the reliability of the configuration process by building an automatic feedback path from the configuration validation module to the code generation module, and 3) LLMs find-tuned for domain-specific tasks.

2. Related Works
LLMs have demonstrated strong generalization ability in huge variety of networking tasks. There are some recent papers trying to use LLM for routing configurations. For example, NetConfEval [1] showcases how LLMs can be applied in network configuration by translating high-level requirements into low-level and router-executable configuration codes. NetConfEval relies on straightforward prompt interactions with off-the-shelf LLMs. However, since models like GPT4 are not specifically designed for networking tasks, the methodology in NetConfEval is inefficient and prone to error. Furthermore, there are privacy and network security concerns when uploading the expected network setup to the commercial platform provided by OpenAI. This paper is a fundamental redesign of the LLM-assisted routing configuration process, aiming to significantly enhance the efficiency and reliability of the process. We will also address the privacy issue by fine-tuning open-sourced Llama-3 models and deployed them locally.
SwitchV [2] focuses on the validation of software-defined network (SDN) switch configurations using fuzzing and symbolic execution to identify bugs. The sprite of automatic networking bug detection presented in SwitchV (mainly in layer 2) will be adapted in this project to provide quick validation and error-checking on the generated routing configuration code (both in layer 2 and layer 3), thus ensuring the robustness and reliability of the resulting router configuration code.

3. Tentative Thoughts for Further Discussions
With a general system setup illustrated in Figure 1, I found several interesting problems that are worth exploring.
 
1.	To build a reference database that contains previously obtained network configuration codes and associated network topological graphs . And to develop a topology-based similarity measurement scheme that can be deployed in the RAG module so that the most relevant previously generated code can be reused for generating the configuration of a new networking setup.
2.	To fine-tune an LLM that can cooperate with the RAG module and make changes according to the model's difference analysis. This LLM will be used for generating the network configuration code given: 1) the most relevant code retrieved by the RAG module, 2) the difference between the target network's topology and the topology associated with the retrieved code, 3) additional user specifications (if any). 
 
3.	To adapt the network validation scheme reported in SwitchV [2] to this work. In [4], the proposed methodology can automatically validate the layer 2 configuration within a LAN. This project aims to extend the scheme for both the switching configuration and the routing configuration, i.e., develop a "layer 2 + layer 3" automatic validation tool.
4.	Given the error checking result, we need an LLM that can revise the network configuration code according to the error feedback from the validation module. The modification module should interact with the validation module in an iterative manner to ensure the reliability of the generated code. The LLM module may also need fine-tuning so that it can better understand the automated validation tool (i.e., SwitchV). 

